  DETAILED for Paley primes p = 3, 7, 11:

  p = 3:
    QRs  = [1]
    NQRs = [2]
    Product of QRs  = 1,  ln(product)/2 = 0.00000000
    Product of NQRs = 2,  ln(product)/2 = 0.34657359
    Sum of QR rapidities  = 0.00000000
    Sum of NQR rapidities = 0.34657359
    (p-1)! = 2
    product(QR) * product(NQR) = 2 = (p-1)!? True
    Total rapidity = ln((p-1)!)/2 = 0.34657359
    = rapidity(QRs) + rapidity(NQRs) = 0.34657359

  p = 7:
    QRs  = [1, 2, 4]
    NQRs = [3, 5, 6]
    Product of QRs  = 8,  ln(product)/2 = 1.03972077
    Product of NQRs = 90,  ln(product)/2 = 2.24990484
    Sum of QR rapidities  = 1.03972077
    Sum of NQR rapidities = 2.24990484
    (p-1)! = 720
    product(QR) * product(NQR) = 720 = (p-1)!? True
    Total rapidity = ln((p-1)!)/2 = 3.28962561
    = rapidity(QRs) + rapidity(NQRs) = 3.28962561

  p = 11:
    QRs  = [1, 3, 4, 5, 9]
    NQRs = [2, 6, 7, 8, 10]
    Product of QRs  = 540,  ln(product)/2 = 3.14578457
    Product of NQRs = 6720,  ln(product)/2 = 4.40642172
    Sum of QR rapidities  = 3.14578457
    Sum of NQR rapidities = 4.40642172
    (p-1)! = 3628800
    product(QR) * product(NQR) = 3628800 = (p-1)!? True
    Total rapidity = ln((p-1)!)/2 = 7.55220629
    = rapidity(QRs) + rapidity(NQRs) = 7.55220629

  ASYMPTOTIC: As p -> infinity,
    avg rapidity of QRs  ~ (1/|QR|) * sum_{a in QR} ln(a)/2
    avg rapidity of NQRs ~ (1/|NQR|) * sum_{a in NQR} ln(a)/2
  Since |QR| = |NQR| = (p-1)/2 and sum_all ln(a)/2 = ln((p-1)!)/2,
  the TOTAL sum is fixed. The question is: do QRs cluster low or high?

