  COLLATZ RAPIDITY DRIFT THEOREM:
  If the trajectory visits k odd numbers in N total steps,
  then N-k are even steps (dividing by 2), giving:
    net rapidity change = k * ln(3)/2 - (N-k) * ln(2)/2 + corrections
  For the trajectory to reach 1 (rapidity 0 from rapidity ln(n)/2),
  we need: k * ln(3) < (N-k) * ln(2) + ln(n)
  i.e., k/N < ln(2)/ln(3) + ln(n)/(N*ln(3))
  ln(2)/ln(3) = 0.6309297536
  So asymptotically, the fraction of odd steps must be < 0.6309...

  For n=27: 41 odd steps out of 111 = 0.369369
    Compare ln(2)/ln(3) = 0.630930

======================================================================
5. PRIME GAPS IN RAPIDITY SPACE
======================================================================

  Generated 9592 primes up to 99991.

  PRIME RAPIDITY GAPS: delta_n = ln(p_{n+1})/2 - ln(p_n)/2 = ln(p_{n+1}/p_n)/2
  Cramer bound on arithmetic gap: g_n <= C * (ln p_n)^2
  Rapidity version: delta_n ~ g_n / (2*p_n) <= C * (ln p_n)^2 / (2*p_n)

        Rapidity range   Max rap gap             At primes   Arith gap  Cramer bnd
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  rap =   0.3466 (     2)    0.20273255  (       2,        3)           1    0.120113
  rap =   0.5493 (     3)    0.25541281  (       3,        5)           2    0.201158

  ASYMPTOTIC: rapidity gaps DECREASE as 1/p on average.
  The rapidity prime 'density' INCREASES: d(rapidity)/dn ~ 1/(2n) for nth number,
  and by PNT the average arithmetic gap at p is ln(p), so avg rapidity gap ~ ln(p)/(2p).

  Average rapidity gap by magnitude range:
