======================================================================
7. SQRT RAPIDITY AND PERFECT SQUARE DETECTION
======================================================================

  rapidity(n) = ln(n)/2
  rapidity(n^2) = ln(n^2)/2 = ln(n) = 2 * rapidity(n)
  rapidity(sqrt(n)) = ln(sqrt(n))/2 = ln(n)/4 = rapidity(n)/2

  PERFECT SQUARES have rapidity = 2 * rapidity(sqrt).
  In other words, n is a perfect square iff rapidity(n) is in the set
  {2*rapidity(k) : k >= 1} = {ln(k) : k >= 1}.

  Since rapidity(n) = ln(n)/2, this means n is a perfect square iff
  there exists integer k with ln(n)/2 = ln(k), i.e., n = k^2.
  Tautological? Not quite -- the RAPIDITY LATTICE tells us more.

  THE RAPIDITY LATTICE of perfect k-th powers:
  rapidity(n^k) = k * rapidity(n)
  So perfect k-th powers form a SUBLATTICE of rapidity space
  with spacing k times the fundamental.

